  <title id="bdlmeanfieldvi-title">Mean-field Gaussian variational approximation of a Bayesian linear regression compared with the exact posterior</title>
  <desc id="bdlmeanfieldvi-desc">Left: in the plane of intercept b and slope w, the exact posterior two-standard-deviation ellipse is long, narrow and tilted, because the two parameters have correlation minus 0.86. The mean-field ellipse has the same center but is axis-aligned and only about as wide as the narrow direction of the exact ellipse, so its marginal standard deviations are about half the exact ones. Right: standard deviation of the regression function against the input from minus 2 to 6, with the range of the training inputs, about 0.1 to 3.8, shaded. The exact curve has a minimum of about 0.11 inside the data range and rises to above 0.4 at both ends; the mean-field curve is flatter, higher than the exact curve over most of the data range and much lower outside it.</desc>
